00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:04.940 align:start position:0%
the beauty of the wave equation Mehta  fists itself in several ways it is  mathematically simple elegant and has  helped to unveil a new understanding of  the physical world classically a  particle can be described by saying that  at a time T it is at a point X in space  and through the equations of motion at  some later time it is moved to a new  position this description has to be  abandoned when describing particles in  quantum mechanics the wave equation  describes how waves propagate it can be  used to describe water waves sound waves  light and radio waves it began with a  simple model of a vibrating violin  string and has become something that can  study a diverse range of phenomena its  link to music has helped us understand  why we can hear sound and why some  combinations are hominis while others  are jarring it is a poster child for the  idea that mathematical principles  developed in one area on their own can  become really helpful in other areas its  beauty comes from its elegance and its  utility when we use the wave equation we  are describing the amplitude of a wave  but when we use the wave equation to  describe a single particle it can become  confusing what this amplitude actually  is with the way of description we no  longer are able to know the precise  location of a particle instead we look  at the probability of finding it at any  given point the amplitude is how likely  it is to be there this leap has both  empowered and haunted quantum mechanics  ever since and has surrendered our world  to being built on a foundation of  uncertainty
 

